      title: "Writing Oneself into Humanity",
      body: `## The genre of the slave narrative

Equiano's *Interesting Narrative* was the most influential of a growing body of autobiographies by formerly enslaved people, which would become, in the nineteenth century, the dominant form of African American writing. Historians estimate that more than a hundred book-length slave narratives were published before the Civil War, and thousands more shorter accounts were recorded in newspapers, pamphlets, and interviews. In the **1930s**, the **Federal Writers' Project** of the Works Progress Administration interviewed more than two thousand formerly enslaved people, then in their eighties and nineties, preserving their memories in some ten thousand pages of transcripts.

## The frame

The slave narratives were usually published with the help of white abolitionists, who wrote prefaces vouching for the truth of the story and the character of the author, and appendices of documents. The scholar **Robert Stepto**, in *From Behind the Veil* (1979), described these as the "authenticating documents" that framed the narrative, and argued that the history of the genre is the history of Black writers' struggle to take control of their own stories from their white sponsors. The critic **John Sekora** called the white preface a "black message in a white envelope."

The frame reflected the reality that Black writers' testimony was doubted. Narratives were attacked by defenders of slavery as fabrications, exaggerations, or the work of white abolitionist ghostwriters. Some were, in fact, ghostwritten or heavily edited; others were fabricated outright, such as *The Narrative of James Williams* (1838), which was withdrawn after Southern critics showed its details to be false. These controversies made the claim "Written by Himself" all the more important.

## Common features

Despite their individual differences, the slave narratives share many conventions. The scholar **James Olney**, in an essay of **1984**, listed them: a first sentence beginning "I was born …," followed by a place but often not a date, since many enslaved people did not know their birthdays; an account of parentage, often including a white father; a description of a cruel master or mistress and of whippings; an account of the struggle to learn to read; a description of a "Christian" master who was worse than a non-Christian one; accounts of slave auctions and the separation of families; the attempt to escape, sometimes failed; the eventual successful escape; the taking of a new name; and reflections on slavery as an institution.

Olney argued that these conventions made the narratives similar, even formulaic. But the best of them—Equiano, Douglass, Jacobs—use the conventions brilliantly, and transform them into works of art.

## The narratives and American literature

The slave narratives are now recognized as one of the founding genres of American literature. They influenced the work of Harriet Beecher Stowe, whose *Uncle Tom's Cabin* drew on the narrative of **Josiah Henson**; Mark Twain, whose *Adventures of Huckleberry Finn* (1884) follows an escaping slave, Jim, down the Mississippi; and, in the twentieth century, a series of "neo-slave narratives," novels that reimagine slavery from the inside: **Margaret Walker**'s *Jubilee* (1966), **Ishmael Reed**'s *Flight to Canada* (1976), **Toni Morrison**'s *Beloved* (1987), **Charles Johnson**'s *Middle Passage* (1990), **Edward P. Jones**'s *The Known World* (2003), and **Colson Whitehead**'s *The Underground Railroad* (2016).

## The question of voice

A central question of this course is the question of **voice**: how could people who had been denied the right to speak make themselves heard, and in what language? The first Black writers had to write in the language of their oppressors—English—and in the literary forms of their oppressors—the heroic couplet, the spiritual autobiography, the sentimental novel. They had to address white readers, whose sympathy they needed, while speaking truths those readers did not want to hear. And they had to prove, again and again, that they were capable of writing at all.

The oral tradition offered another resource: a voice that came from the community itself, in its own idiom, with its own forms of indirection, double meaning, and wit. Much of the history of African American literature can be read as the story of the meeting, and sometimes the conflict, between these two voices: the written and the spoken, the formal and the vernacular, the voice addressed to white readers and the voice addressed to Black ones. We will see this meeting in Douglass's account of the songs of the plantation, in Jacobs's careful negotiation with her white female readers, in Dunbar's dialect poems, and finally in Du Bois's *Souls of Black Folk*, where every chapter begins with a bar of the sorrow songs.

## Signifyin(g)

Henry Louis Gates Jr. gave a name to the distinctive way Black writers responded to the texts of others: "**Signifyin(g)**," a term drawn from African American vernacular, where "signifying" means a form of verbal play—teasing, indirection, repetition with a difference. The trickster figure of the **Signifying Monkey**, from Black folklore, outwits the lion by playing with language. In *The Signifying Monkey* (1988), Gates argued that Black writers constantly "signify" upon the texts that came before them—repeating, revising, and parodying them—both the white texts that excluded them and the Black texts that came before. The tradition is thus in constant dialogue with itself. Equiano revises Gronniosaw's talking book; Douglass revises Equiano; Jacobs revises Douglass; and Du Bois gathers them all.

> **Pause and consider.** The slave narratives were often framed by white prefaces vouching for their truth. What does it mean for a writer's story to need someone else's authentication?`,
